From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: Delegate cross-uid process operations to a per-user agent

Stained Glass ADR 0014 (multi-user debt D16, D19). The machine-level server
runs as the SYSTEM account, so the kernel refuses it ptrace/tgkill on an
ordinary user's processes: ReadProcessMemory/WriteProcessMemory across
processes, DebugActiveProcess (which writes the debuggee's PEB), thread signals
for asynchronous procedure calls, and sched_setaffinity all failed with
ERROR_ACCESS_DENIED for ordinary users, while working for the server's own
account.

The server now delegates each of those to a small unprivileged agent
(sg-session's sg-procagent) that runs as the target's user and performs the
operation on that user's own processes, where the kernel allows it. Nothing
gains a capability -- the agent can only touch its own user's processes, as
that user already could. Same-uid operations (the server's own account, e.g. a
program elevated to SYSTEM through the broker) are still done directly, and the
whole mechanism is inert on an ordinary, non-system prefix.

  - read_process_memory / write_process_memory / send_thread_signal
    (server/ptrace.c) and set_thread_affinity (server/thread.c) delegate when
    the target's peer_uid is not the server's uid.
  - The server connects to <prefix>/.sg-procagent.<uid>, requires the path to
    be a socket owned by that uid, and verifies SO_PEERCRED -- so a member of
    the prefix group cannot impersonate another user's agent. The fd is cached
    per uid; a dead or absent agent means the operation fails exactly as it
    does today (no regression when no agent is running).
  - request.c keeps the prefix path (get_config_dir) so the server can form the
    socket path; it is otherwise unused there.

Wire protocol (must match sg-procagent): a fixed request {op, sig, pid, tid,
addr, len} then len payload bytes for a write; a reply {status, len} then len
bytes for a read.

Tested as a second Unix user against a shared server with the agent running:
ntdll:file and ntdll:info go from 6 and 54 failures to 0 (matching the
server's-own-user baseline), the loader suite's cross-process ReadProcessMemory
failures are gone, and kernel32:process/loader match the baseline exactly. No
change for the server's own user. Debug registers (hardware breakpoints) are
not yet delegated; software breakpoints, which go through WriteProcessMemory,
work.
